@4677[9]. "Bread" = the good of love, or what is celestial. References. @4844[11]. @5893. @5915. @9295[2]. @AE 175[8]. @480. @617[10].

@4735[2]. Divine celestial love is signified by "the flesh" or "bread" in the Holy Supper. Illustrated.

@4745. "They sat down to eat bread" (Genesis 37:25) = the appropriation of evil from falsity. Explained.

@4876[4]. "The staff of bread" = support and power from the good of love. @9028[end]. @9323[2].

@4984. "He knew not anything with him except the bread which he did eat" (Genesis 39:6) = that good was thence appropriated.

@5360. Bread corresponds to celestial love.

@5363. "In all the land of Egypt there was bread" (Genesis 41:54) = remains from the multiplied truths from good. Explained.

@5365. "The people cried to Pharaoh for bread" (Genesis 41:55) = the need of good for truth. Explained.

@5405. In the Ancient Church bread was broken when it was given to another, and by this was signified to communicate from one's own, and to appropriate what is good from one's own, and so effect mutual love. Explained, Illustrated. @AE 617[21].

@5673. "They heard that they should eat bread there" (Genesis 43:25) = the perception that good would be adjoined to truths... "Bread" = the good of love. References.

@5698. "He said, Set on bread" (Genesis 43:31) = the perception of conjunction through the medium with truths in the Natural. Explained.

