@4327. Their thoughts are full of butchery.

@ISB 16[end]. These are like butchers...

Butler.; Pincerna.

@AC 5072. The sensuous things which are subordinate to the intellectual part are represented by "the butler of the king of Egypt"... That these are retained is represented by the butler being restored to his post.

@5077. "The butler of the king of Egypt" (Genesis 40) = with those things in the body which are subject to the intellectual part. "A butler" = the external Sensual, or Sensual of the body which is subordinate or subject to the intellectual part of the internal man. Explained. @5082. @5094. @5128. @5163. @5227.

@5095. "The butler" and "the baker" represent the Lord as to those things which are of the external Natural. @5118.

@5110. "The prince of the cupbearers" = the Sensual subject to the intellectual part in general, and which heretofore has been rejected.

@5165. "He brought back the prince of the cupbearers upon his pledging" = that the sensuous things of the intellectual part were received and subordinated. Explained.

@AE 655[7]. "A butler" = the truth of doctrine.

Butter.; Butyrum.

@AC 195[end]. "Rivers of honey and butter" (Job 20:17) = spiritual and celestial things, which reasoners will not see.

@680[end]. To "eat honey and butter" (Isaiah 7:15) = what is celestial spiritual.

@1002. In the most ancient time they ate... butter, etc.

@2184. "Butter" (Genesis 18:8) = the celestial of the Rational. "Butter," in the Word = what is celestial, from its fatness. Illustrated.

@--. "Butter and honey shall He eat"... "Butter" = His Celestial; and "honey," that which is from the Celestial. @5620[2].

@--[2]. "Milk" (Isaiah 7:22) = spiritual good; "butter," celestial good; and "honey," the happiness thence derived. @5620[3].

@--[3]. "Butter of the herd" (Deuteronomy 32:14) = what is celestial natural.

@SE 1161. That the spiritual are averse to butter. @1162. @3894.

@1163. Butter is of the celestials...

@AE 304[45]. "The butter and honey which He shall eat" = the goods of love; "butter," the good of celestial and spiritual love; "honey," the good of natural love.

@314[7]. "Butter of the herd, and milk of the flock" = the good of truth external and internal.

@537[10]. "Butters" (Psalms 55:21) = the good of external affection; "oil," the good of internal affection.

@617[8]. The appropriation of Divine good spiritual and natural as to the Human is meant by "butter and honey shall He eat;" Divine good spiritual by "butter," and Divine good natural by "honey."

@--[9]. "Butter and honey shall everyone eat that is left in the land"... "Butter and honey" = spiritual good and natural good.

@619[4]. "Butter and honey shall He eat"... "Butter" = the delight of spiritual good; and "honey" the delight of natural good; thus the Divine Spiritual and the Divine Natural; thus His Human interiorly and exteriorly. @--[5].

Butterfly.; Papilio.

@AC 8848. They likened these things to worms (becoming) butterflies. @3000. @DLW 354[end]. @CL 418. @TCR 106[2].

@TCR 35. Some they regard as butterflies...

@361[end]. His external may be likened to butterflies...

@375[end]. They may be compared to butterflies in the air...

Buttock.; Clunis.

